  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Peckoltia brevis (LaMonte, 1935)</li><li>Common names: LDA078, Purus Tiger Pleco</li><li>Synonym: Hemiancistrus brevis</li><li>Type locality: near Sena Madureira, near the mouth of the River Macaua, a tributary of the Rio Iaco, itself a tributary of the Rio Purus, Amazonas, Brazil.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<p>South America: Purus River basin, Brazil.</p><p>IUCN Red List category: Least Concern (last assessed 2020).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<ul><li>pH: 5.8–7.8</li><li>Temperature: 22–26°C</li><li>dGH: 15–28</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<p>Rock piles and wood tangles. Plants are not vital.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<p>Omnivorous with a tendency towards meaty foods. Not an effective algae eater. Well fed individuals will not damage most plants.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<p>Peaceful if a little territorial. As a rough guide, give each fish a 30 cm square area well broken up with decorations.</p><p>A medium sized pleco that can be combined with a very wide array of tankmates due to its unfussy nature. Perfectly safe with small fishes and shrimp, or robust enough to withstand the attention of all but the largest cichlids.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<p>Males develop small teeth-like odontodes which adorn the leading pectoral fin rays and the back half of the body. Females remain unadorned and have a wider girth across the body, most evident when the area just behind the pectoral fins is viewed from above.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<p>Cave spawner.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1339",
      "html": "<p>American Museum Novitates No. 784, pp 3, Fig. 2.</p>"
    }
  ],
